-- What a move costs, as declarations rather than as branches of one search.
--
-- Sections are `docs/rules/rulemanual.txt`: §5-3 paying an energy cost and
-- §8-3-2-3 doing it in the Main Phase, §1-2-3 the specified (coloured) half of
-- a cost, §1-14-2 energy markers standing in for energy of the Leader's
-- colour, §3-8-2 the player's free choice of which energy to rest, §13-4 a
-- Unison's marker cost, §20-5 an X price, §20-19 a card rested in the Energy
-- Area's place, §21-3 life as a price, and §22-30 [Warrior of Universe 7].
--
-- In the legacy engine payment is one function, `planPayment` in
-- `engine/state.ts`, with a branch inside it for each of those: the coloured
-- orbs, the either-orbs, X, the cards that may stand in for energy, and
-- [Warrior of Universe 7] clearing the colours outright. It plays this game
-- correctly and it can only ever play this game — a second game with a
-- different resource has nowhere to say so. Here each is a `DEFINE COST`, and
-- `vm/costs.ts` is one planner over the **declared** kinds (#148).
--
-- Two lines carry the whole of what an interpreter needs to charge a price it
-- has never seen:
--
--   `consumes:`  the resource it takes — `energy`, `markers`, `life`, `mode`,
--                `cards`, or `unreadable` for the half of a printed price no
--                engine can charge itself. The planner switches on this word
--                and never on the declaration's *name*, so a second game's
--                `DEFINE COST mana / consumes: energy` is charged by the same
--                search this file's `energy` is.
--   `amount:`    the **card attribute** the price's amount is read off, when
--                the move asking for it names a card (§20-21). `ACTION play
--                COST [energy]` passes no arguments, so the number comes from
--                the card being paid for, and this line is where the game says
--                which number that is. It names the *derived* attribute
--                (`costOf`, never `energyCost`), so every reduction in force
--                reaches a play's price through the declared `layers:` rather
--                than through a second path.
--   `asks:`      how the payment puts its question (§3-8-2). `choice` is a
--                price the payer is asked about whenever more than one
--                genuinely different way to pay it exists — which is the
--                `payCost` prompt the board already answers; `nothing` is a
--                price with only ever one way to pay, which is settled without
--                a question.
--
-- The `DO` program says two things at once, and both are read: its **target**
-- is the pool the price is paid out of (the active cards in your Energy Area,
-- the cards in your life), and the **op** is what paying does to what was
-- taken from it. How *many* is the price's own amount and is never in the
-- program — an action asks for a price by name and the amount comes off the
-- card being paid for.
--
-- Two gaps are written here rather than papered over. `TAKES` names what each
-- price is given, but `ACTION ... COST [energy]` passes no arguments, so where
-- the values come from is the *thing being paid for*: a price paid for a card
-- reads its amount off the card through `amount:` above, and a price paid for a
-- **skill line** has it bound in by the interpreter that knows which line it is
-- (#147, `vm/costs.ts`'s `BoundAmounts`) — which is where `marker` and `life`
-- get their numbers and `text` gets bound away on a skill whose whole cost is
-- orbs. `payWith` still has nothing to bind it anywhere and is refused by name.
-- And an **either-orb** ("{Red/Blue}", one orb payable with any one of the
-- listed colours, §22-13) has no parameter type that can name it: `colors` is a
-- flat list and the language knows no list of alternatives. The planner reads them off the price
-- and charges them exactly; the parameter list below cannot say so, the way
-- `attributes.rules` cannot say what is printed on a card's back side.

DEFINE COST energy                                                        -- §5-3
  TAKES (total: amount, orbs: colors, x: amount)
  consumes: energy
  amount: "costOf"
  asks: choice
  DO {
    switchMode(target: IN you.energy active, mode: rest)
  }
  text: "the price is paid by switching that many active cards in your Energy Area to Rest Mode, the coloured orbs matched in colour; an energy marker pays one energy of your Leader's colour in a card's place (5-3-3, 1-2-3, 1-14-2)"

-- The other half of a Z-card's price (§5-4). It is a `cards` price like
-- `payWith` below and the planner tells the two apart the way it tells any two
-- prices apart — by reading the `DO`, never the name: this one's target names
-- a **place** (`IN you.zEnergy`), so the cards come out of that pool and go
-- where the op says; `payWith`'s names cards the price was handed, so they are
-- rested where they stand. 5-4-3: the Z-Energy is spent, not rested — the
-- cards are placed in the Drop Area — which is why the op is a move.
DEFINE COST zEnergy                                                       -- §5-4
  TAKES (n: amount)
  consumes: cards
  amount: "zEnergyCostOf"
  asks: nothing
  DO {
    moveTo(target: IN you.zEnergy, to: drop)
  }
  text: "a Z-card's Z-Energy price: that many cards are placed from the Z-Energy Area in the Drop Area, on top of the card's energy cost (5-4, 16-2-1)"

DEFINE COST marker                                                        -- §13-4
  TAKES (n: amount)
  consumes: markers
  asks: nothing
  DO {
    addMarker(target: [self], n: $n)
  }
  text: "the price is paid with the markers on the card whose skill is being used: a negative number removes that many, and the card may use no further marker skill this turn (13-4, 1-11)"

DEFINE COST life                                                          -- §21-3
  TAKES (n: amount)
  consumes: life
  asks: nothing
  DO {
    moveTo(target: IN you.life, to: drop)
  }
  text: "the price is paid by placing that many cards from your life in your Drop Area (21-3)"

DEFINE COST rest                                                          -- §8-4-1
  TAKES ()
  consumes: mode
  asks: nothing
  DO {
    switchMode(target: [self], mode: rest)
  }
  text: "the price is paid by switching the card itself to Rest Mode, which a card already in Rest Mode cannot do (1-10-1)"

DEFINE COST payWith                                                       -- §20-19
  TAKES (cards: selector)
  consumes: cards
  asks: choice
  DO {
    switchMode(target: FROM $cards, mode: rest)
  }
  text: "cards outside the Energy Area that this price may be paid with instead, each rested where it stands and counting as energy of the colours the price says (20-19)"

-- The price the engine does not charge, and the reason it is a declaration at
-- all: a move whose price has no `DEFINE COST` would be offered for free, and
-- a move whose price is *declared unreadable* is refused with an `unread`
-- requirement naming the card — the same answer the legacy engine gives for a
-- skill whose cost the compiler could not read.
DEFINE COST text                                                          -- §4-3-3
  TAKES (printed: string)
  consumes: unreadable
  asks: nothing
  DO {}
  text: "the price as printed, kept for the part no engine can charge itself; a move that asks for it is refused rather than offered free (4-3-3)"
